-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 - Chapters 4 and 4A (ss 76-106A): the ROLL-UP of
-- the general exceptions and the right of private defence, and the transfer of
-- defences under s 26G(5) and s 301(2).
--
-- Source: Penal Code 1871, 2020 Revised Edition, SSO "Current version as at
-- 09 Sep 2026" (inputs/PC1871.txt).
--
-- The sections themselves are encoded in pc-exceptions-general.l4 (Chapter 4)
-- and pc-exceptions-private-defence.l4 (Chapter 4A); read their headers first.
-- This module adds only:
--
--   * `a general exception applies` f - one ladder over a record nesting every
--     exception's facts record, each drawn as one box (s 6: every offence is
--     "understood subject to" Chapters 4 and 4A). It is what the charge sheet
--     reads beside `offence under s N` at integration. It is NOT a condition
--     of any charge: CPC 2010 s 123(5) asserts the conditions that constitute
--     the offence, and Evidence Act 1893 s 107 puts the burden of an exception
--     on the accused and tells the court to presume its absence.
--
--   * s 26G(5) and s 301(2), which notes/PLAN.md §5 gives this group to wire:
--     where the act or effect concerned a person or thing other than the one
--     the accused meant, he may rely on any defence or exception as though it
--     had concerned the intended one.
--
-- ss 79A, 86, 90 and 91 are not in the roll-up: s 79A says when a mistake of
-- law is NOT a defence, s 86 is the effect of s 85, s 90 defines "consent",
-- and s 91 narrows ss 87-89 (it is read inside them). ss 97-106A are parts of
-- s 96 and are read inside it.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pc-domain`
IMPORT `pc-general`
IMPORT `pc-exceptions-general`
IMPORT `pc-exceptions-private-defence`

§ `Chapters 4 and 4A - the exceptions taken together`

§§ `s 6 - every offence is understood subject to the General Exceptions and the Right of Private Defence`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 6; Chapters 4 and 4A
DECLARE `General Exception Facts` HAS
    `bound or justified by law`     IS A `Bound or Justified by Law Facts` @desc s 76 - act done by a person bound, or justified, by law
    `act of a judge`                IS A `Judicial Act Facts`              @desc s 77 - act of a judge when acting judicially
    `act under a court order`       IS A `Court Order Facts`               @desc s 78 - act done pursuant to the judgment or order of a court of justice
    `mistake of fact`               IS A `Mistake of Fact Facts`           @desc s 79 - act done by mistake of fact, believing oneself bound or justified by law
    `accident`                      IS A `Accident Facts`                  @desc s 80 - accident in the doing of a lawful act
    `necessity`                     IS A `Necessity Facts`                 @desc s 81 - act likely to cause harm, done to prevent other harm
    `act of a child`                IS A `Child Facts`                     @desc ss 82, 83 - act of a child
    `unsoundness of mind`           IS A `Unsoundness of Mind Facts`       @desc s 84 - act of a person of unsound mind
    `intoxication`                  IS A `Intoxication Facts`              @desc s 85 - intoxication
    `consent to harm`               IS A `Consent to Harm Facts`           @desc ss 87, 88, 89 (read with s 91) - acts done by consent, or by a guardian's consent for a person's benefit
    `benefit without consent`       IS A `Benefit Without Consent Facts`   @desc s 92 - act done in good faith for a person's benefit without consent
    `communication in good faith`   IS A `Communication Facts`             @desc s 93 - communication made in good faith
    `compulsion by threats`         IS A `Compulsion by Threats Facts`     @desc s 94 - act to which a person is compelled by threats
    `slight harm`                   IS A `Slight Harm Facts`               @desc s 95 - act causing slight harm
    `private defence`               IS A `Private Defence Facts`           @desc s 96 - act done in the exercise of the right of private defence

@export Whether any general exception (Chapter 4) or the right of private defence (Chapter 4A) of the Penal Code 1871 applies to the act
GIVEN f IS A `General Exception Facts` @desc The facts bearing on each general exception and on private defence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a general exception applies` f IF
        "s 6: every definition of an offence, every penal provision, and every illustration of every such definition or penal provision, shall be understood subject to the exceptions contained in the Chapters entitled 'General Exceptions' and 'Right of Private Defence'"
    AND (    `exception under s 76` (f's `bound or justified by law`)
         OR  `exception under s 77` (f's `act of a judge`)
         OR  `exception under s 78` (f's `act under a court order`)
         OR  `exception under s 79` (f's `mistake of fact`)
         OR  `exception under s 80` (f's `accident`)
         OR  `exception under s 81` (f's `necessity`)
         OR  `exception under s 82` (f's `act of a child`)
         OR  `exception under s 83` (f's `act of a child`)
         OR  `exception under s 84` (f's `unsoundness of mind`)
         OR  `exception under s 85` (f's `intoxication`)
         OR  `exception under s 87` (f's `consent to harm`)
         OR  `exception under s 88` (f's `consent to harm`)
         OR  `exception under s 89` (f's `consent to harm`)
         OR  `exception under s 92` (f's `benefit without consent`)
         OR  `exception under s 93` (f's `communication in good faith`)
         OR  `exception under s 94` (f's `compulsion by threats`)
         OR  `exception under s 95` (f's `slight harm`)
         OR  `exception under s 96` (f's `private defence`) )

§§ `ss 26G(5) and 301(2) - relying on a defence as though the act concerned the intended person or thing`

-- 26G(5) Where the circumstances mentioned in subsection (2)(a) and (b) exist,
-- the accused person may rely on any defence or exception in law as though the
-- act or effect mentioned in subsection (2)(a) concerned the person or thing
-- the accused person believed or intended that act or effect to concern.
--
-- 301(2) To avoid doubt, in the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1), the
-- accused person may rely on any defence or exception in law as though the
-- accused person had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or
-- knew himself to be likely to cause.
--
-- "May rely on" ADDS a route: the defences open as the facts actually were
-- remain open. The two "would apply" leaves are what `a general exception
-- applies` (or any other defence in law - a special exception such as s 300's)
-- answers on the facts re-stated with the intended person or thing; the
-- charge sheet evaluates it twice at integration. s 26G(1) excludes offences
-- to which s 301(1) applies, so at most one of the two routes can be open.
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 26G(2), 26G(5), 301(1), 301(2)
DECLARE `Transferred Defence Facts` HAS
    `a defence or exception in law applies as the act or effect actually was`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc On the facts as they actually were, does any defence or exception in law apply - a general exception or private defence (`a general exception applies`), or a special exception in the offence's own section?
    `the physical elements of the offence included the doing of an act or the causing of an effect concerning a person or thing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the physical elements of the offence include doing an act, or causing an effect, concerning a person or thing (s 26G(2)(a))?
    `the accused person believed or intended that the act or effect would concern a different person or thing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc When the act was done or the effect caused, did the accused believe or intend that it would concern a different person or thing (s 26G(2)(b))?
    `a defence or exception in law would apply had the act or effect concerned the person or thing the accused person believed or intended it to concern`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would any defence or exception in law apply if the act or effect had concerned the person or thing the accused believed or intended it to concern (s 26G(5))? For example, private defence against B, where the stone meant for B struck C.
    `the accused person, by doing anything which he intends or knows to be likely to cause death, commits culpable homicide by causing the death of a person whose death he neither intends nor knows himself to be likely to cause`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are these the circumstances of s 301(1) - culpable homicide of a person whose death the accused neither intended nor knew himself likely to cause, by an act he intended or knew to be likely to cause death?
    `a defence or exception in law would apply had he caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would any defence or exception in law apply if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause (s 301(2))?

@export Whether the accused person may rely on a defence or exception as though the act or effect concerned the person or thing he believed or intended it to concern, under section 26G(5) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Transferred Defence Facts` @desc The person or thing actually concerned, the one the accused meant, and the defences on each
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused person may rely on a defence under section 26G(5)` f IF
        "(5) Where the circumstances mentioned in subsection (2)(a)"
    AND f's `the physical elements of the offence included the doing of an act or the causing of an effect concerning a person or thing`
    AND "and (b)"
         ... f's `the accused person believed or intended that the act or effect would concern a different person or thing`
    AND "exist, the accused person may rely on any defence or exception in law as though the act or effect mentioned in subsection (2)(a) concerned the person or thing the accused person believed or intended that act or effect to concern"
         ... f's `a defence or exception in law would apply had the act or effect concerned the person or thing the accused person believed or intended it to concern`

@export Whether the accused person may rely on a defence or exception as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself likely to cause, under section 301(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Transferred Defence Facts` @desc The person actually killed, the one whose death was intended or known likely, and the defences on each
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused person may rely on a defence under section 301(2)` f IF
        "(2) To avoid doubt, in the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)"
    AND f's `the accused person, by doing anything which he intends or knows to be likely to cause death, commits culpable homicide by causing the death of a person whose death he neither intends nor knows himself to be likely to cause`
    AND "the accused person may rely on any defence or exception in law as though the accused person had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause"
         ... f's `a defence or exception in law would apply had he caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`

@export Whether a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person, as the facts were or under section 26G(5) or 301(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Transferred Defence Facts` @desc The defences on the facts as they were, and as though the intended person or thing had been concerned
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` f IF
        f's `a defence or exception in law applies as the act or effect actually was`
    OR  `the accused person may rely on a defence under section 26G(5)` f
    OR  `the accused person may rely on a defence under section 301(2)` f
